dangerous**, possibly letting them win by completing kong or giving them extra points.\n- **Play B5**: Similar to B4/T4/T5, mid-number tile. Only one B5 discarded so far. Could be part of middle sequences or All Fives pattern.\n\nKey considerations:\n- The next player has exposed [W8, W8, W8] and [F3, F3, F3], indicating strong focus on wind tiles \u2014 likely going for a wind-heavy hand (e.g., Big/Little Three/Four Winds). Discarding F3 or W8 would directly feed them.\n- You have a concealed pung of **T6**, which is powerful. Also working toward possible chows in T-suit: T4-T5-T6-T6-T6-T7.\n- Possible developing pattern: **All Simples** (if no terminals/honors), **Concealed Hand**, **Pure Shifted Chows**, or even **All Fives** if structured properly.\n- Honors in hand: F3 (seat wind), F4 (prevailing wind), both worth keeping for 2-point patterns each.\n\nBest candidates to discard: **T2**, **B4**, **B5**, or **T7** \u2014 weakest connectors.\n\nAmong these, **T2** stands out as least useful \u2014 isolated, not connecting to any sequence, and not contributing to symmetry or common patterns.\n\nReason:  \nDiscarding **T2** minimizes risk to opponents (no evidence of them collecting early bamboos), removes an isolated tile that doesn\u2019t integrate into current structure, and keeps key elements: T4-T5-T6-T6-T6-T7 flexibility, potential honor pairs/fans, and safety from feeding active threats (next player\u2019s W8/F3 pungs).\n\nAnswer: Play T2"}
